<LegalPage
  title="Privacy"
  description="What telium.chat collects (almost nothing) and who is responsible for your data on a homeserver."
  updated="2026-11-13"
>
  <p>
    This page covers <strong>telium.chat, the website and the copy of the Telium web client we host</strong>.
    It does not cover any homeserver. Homeservers are run by independent operators, and
    your account, messages and files live on the one you choose.
  </p>

  <h2>What this website collects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No accounts, no sign-up and no cookies.</li>
    <li>No analytics, no advertising, no third-party fonts and no trackers.</li>
    <li>
      Your browser stores two preferences locally, and they never leave your device: your
      theme choice and whether the directory may measure latency to servers.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The site is served as static files by our hosting provider (Cloudflare). Like any web
    host, the provider may keep standard request logs such as IP address and requested
    page. We do not receive or analyse them.
  </p>

  <h2>The directory and latency checks</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={URLS.servers}>server directory</a> lists homeservers that asked to be listed.
    Registration status, versions and uptime come from our own periodic checks of those
    servers' public endpoints. The latency column is measured <strong>by your browser</strong>
    contacting each listed server directly, so each of those servers can see your IP address
    for that request. You can switch this off in the directory; nothing else on the site
    depends on it.
  </p>

  <h2>The hosted web client</h2>
  <p>
    The client at telium.chat is the same static program anyone can host. It runs in your
    browser and talks directly to the homeserver you give it. Your messages, account and
    files are never sent to telium.chat, which only delivers the program itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Your homeserver</h2>
  <p>
    Whoever runs your homeserver controls what is stored about you there, how long it is kept,
    and who can see it. In a federated network, servers that share a guild with yours also
    receive the content and the account identifiers needed for that guild to work. Before
    you sign up, read the privacy policy and terms the operator publishes. Operators are expected to
    publish them; if you can't find any, ask the operator before you trust the server with anything sensitive.
  </p>
  <p>
    Direct messages and calls use end-to-end encryption where your client and the other
    person's client support it. An operator cannot read content that is encrypted end to end,
    but can still see metadata such as who talks to whom and when.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    For this website, open an issue on <a href={URLS.github}>GitHub</a>. For a security
    problem, use the private reporting flow described in the repository's security policy.
    For anything on a homeserver, contact that server's operator (see <a href="/abuse">Abuse and takedowns</a>).
  </p>
</LegalPage>
